{
  "id": "passiv-b1",
  "name": "The passive: present and past simple",
  "level": "B1",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "subtitle": "In the passive the action matters more than who does it: English is spoken here. The bridge was built in 1890. Use am / is / are or was / were and the past participle, and add by + the doer only when it is important.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "is spoken",
    "was built",
    "past participle"
  ],
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-b1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "comic": {
      "title": "What is happening here?",
      "panels": [
        {
          "caption": "Present simple",
          "tag": "{is} / {are} + past participle → true now or usually",
          "left": {
            "name": "quaki",
            "expr": "thinking",
            "say": "Where does this cheese come from?"
          },
          "right": {
            "name": "bruno",
            "expr": "happy",
            "say": "It {is} {made} in Wales."
          }
        },
        {
          "caption": "Past simple",
          "tag": "{was} / {were} + past participle → it happened then",
          "left": {
            "name": "regi",
            "expr": "thinking",
            "say": "Is your house very old?"
          },
          "right": {
            "name": "hamsti",
            "expr": "happy",
            "say": "Yes, it {was} {built} in 1910."
          }
        },
        {
          "caption": "by + doer",
          "tag": "{by} + who did it (only if it matters)",
          "left": {
            "name": "hamsti",
            "expr": "thinking",
            "say": "Who painted this picture?"
          },
          "right": {
            "name": "quaki",
            "expr": "celebrate",
            "say": "It {was} {painted} {by} my sister!"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "Subject (the thing that is affected)"
      },
      {
        "text": "am / is / are · was / were",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "past participle",
        "variant": "prep"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "The bridge <span class=\"vp-verb\">was</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">built</span> in 1890. · English <span class=\"vp-verb\">is</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">spoken</span> here.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>Active</strong>: who does something? <em>A mechanic repairs the car.</em> <strong>Passive</strong>: what happens to the thing? <em>The car is repaired.</em> The object of the active sentence (<em>the car</em>) becomes the subject.",
        "tag": "Active or passive?"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Present simple</strong>: <em>am / is / are</em> + past participle. Use it for facts and habits: <em>The car is repaired here. The windows are cleaned every week. I am paid on Friday.</em> Negative: <em>isn't / aren't</em>. Question: <em>Is the car repaired here?</em>",
        "tag": "Present simple"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Past simple</strong>: <em>was / were</em> + past participle: <em>The house was built in 1910. The windows were cleaned yesterday.</em> Question: <em>When was it built?</em>",
        "tag": "Past simple"
      },
      {
        "html": "Say who does it only when it is <strong>important or new</strong>, with <strong>by</strong>: <em>The Mona Lisa was painted by Leonardo.</em> Often you do not say it, because it is unknown or obvious: <em>My bike was stolen.</em>",
        "tag": "by + doer"
      },
      {
        "html": "The <strong>past participle</strong> of regular verbs ends in <em>-ed</em>: <em>cleaned, painted</em>. Learn the irregular ones: <em>make → made, build → built, write → written, steal → stolen, break → broken</em>.",
        "tag": "Past participle"
      },
      {
        "html": "✗ <em>The house built in 1910.</em> → ✓ <em>The house was built in 1910.</em> (do not forget was / were) · ✗ <em>The window was broke.</em> → ✓ <em>The window was broken.</em> (past participle, not past simple) · ✗ <em>The picture was painted from my sister.</em> → ✓ <em>… by my sister.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Use the passive when the doer is unknown, obvious or not important, and in notices and reports: <em>Smoking is not allowed here.</em> Some phrases are always passive: <em>I was born in Leeds</em> (not <em>I am born</em>). In everyday speech English often prefers the active, so do not use the passive everywhere. The passive exists in other tenses too (<em>is being built, has been built, will be built</em>), but this topic stops at the present and past simple. See <a href=\"/present-simple\">Present simple</a> and <a href=\"/past-simple\">Past simple</a> for the tenses, and <a href=\"/future-forms\">Future forms</a> for will.",
